00:10:34.347 --> 00:10:40.415
sense. The method is fixed five session holds, entry at the next open, stop or horizon

00:10:40.415 --> 00:10:45.355
exits, and no discretionary exits, so you can take the pick, the date, and the stop,

00:10:45.695 --> 00:10:52.435
and reproduce every number. You already hold XEQT in your TFSA, and you add a 20% allocation

00:10:52.435 --> 00:10:59.255
to VGRO after noticing both funds track similar equity heavy mixes. The mechanism that matters

00:10:59.255 --> 00:11:04.855
is correlation. When equities fall together, the combined drawdown stays smaller than a single

00:11:04.855 --> 00:11:11.555
fun position, because the bond sleeve in VGRO offsets equity losses without requiring perfect timing.

00:11:12.275 --> 00:11:16.675
Modern portfolio theory shows this risk reduction happens at the portfolio level,

00:11:17.155 --> 00:11:23.075
even if expected returns stay roughly the same, moving the overall mix closer to the efficient frontier,

00:11:23.615 --> 00:11:29.115
where the highest return is achieved for a given level of risk. A practical check is to run a simple

00:11:29.115 --> 00:11:34.815
Correlation Matrix in a spreadsheet or portfolio tool every quarter, using three years of monthly

00:11:34.815 --> 00:11:37.355
returns for the exchange-traded funds you own.

00:11:37.895 --> 00:11:42.235
Professional managers rebalance when any sleeve drifts more than five percentage points

00:11:42.235 --> 00:11:47.115
from target, and you can copy that threshold rule inside tax-advantaged accounts without

00:11:47.115 --> 00:11:47.755
tax drag.

00:11:48.455 --> 00:11:53.675
Strategic asset allocation sets long-term targets based on risk tolerance and time horizon,

00:11:54.175 --> 00:11:58.835
while tactical shifts respond to shorter-term opportunities such as sector rotation into

00:11:58.835 --> 00:12:05.055
utilities after a rate hike. The role of bonds remains relevant even after 2022 because

00:12:05.055 --> 00:12:09.855
they still provide ballast during equity drawdowns, though you must size the sleeve to your

00:12:09.855 --> 00:12:16.235
actual spending horizon rather than a generic age formula. Canadian examples include XEQT

00:12:16.235 --> 00:12:23.795
for all equity growth, VGRO for a moderate 80-20 mix, and XBAL for a balanced 60-40 approach

00:12:23.795 --> 00:12:29.355
that can be held inside an RRSP, or a first home savings account, or FHSA.

00:12:29.915 --> 00:12:34.935
The common misconception is that adding bonds after 2022 automatically hurts returns.

00:12:35.635 --> 00:12:40.615
The fix is to size the fixed income sleeve to your actual spending horizon, then let the

00:12:40.615 --> 00:12:42.355
allocation do the defensive work.

00:12:42.855 --> 00:12:47.695
Remember, size the bond sleeve to the bills you actually have to pay, not to your birthday.

00:12:47.695 --> 00:12:53.897
